--- PROOF.md ---
VALIDATED

# What is proven

For every finite list `INPUT` of finite ASCII decimal-digit strings, calling
`odd_count(INPUT)` returns a fresh list whose corresponding element is

```text
the number of odd elements Nn the strNng N of the Nnput.
```

where `N` is the number of odd digits in that input string. This is exactly the
result of replacing every `i` in
`the number of odd elements in the string i of the input.` with that count.
The claim covers empty input lists, empty strings, repeated elements, and all
ASCII digit strings of arbitrary finite length. It is a partial-correctness
claim over the supplied reference semantics.

# Formal claim

The target claim is `ODD-COUNT-CORRECT` in module `SPEC` in `spec.k`. It starts
from the supplied semantics' initial configuration, loads the exact translated
`solutionProgram`, calls `odd_count` with `INPUT`, and proves that execution
reaches `ref(0)` with heap location `0` containing
`list(oddCountAppend(.ValSeq, INPUT))`, under `digitStrings(INPUT)`.

`oddCountAppend` recursively maps each input string to `sentenceVal(N)`, with
`N = oddDigits(codesOfVal(V))`. `sentenceVal(N)` is connected to the concrete
runtime string by the guarded equality to `str(sentenceCodes(N))` in
`VERIFICATION-SENTENCE-DEFINITION`; `sentenceCodes` is the exact ASCII encoding
of the required replacement result.

# Proof-extension inventory

| Extension | Purpose | Reads | Writes | Justification |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `oddBit`, `oddDigits` | Count odd ASCII digits | Mathematical integers and finite `IntSeq` values | None | Recursive equations decrease structurally; `oddBit(C) = (C - 48) mod 2` on the guarded digit domain. |
| `codesOfVal`, `digitCodes`, `digitStrings` | State and enforce the formal input domain | Values and finite sequences | None | `codesOfVal` is total through its `owise` case; proof bridges require the positive string/digit guards. |
| `sentenceCodes`, `sentenceVal` | Give an exact, compact proof representation of output strings | Count `N` | None | The final-only guarded rule applies after the fixed string expression evaluates and only when its concrete codes equal `sentenceCodes(N)`. Concretization is `sentenceVal(N) -> str(sentenceCodes(N))`. |
| Inner-loop bridge | Summarize one exact translated inner digit loop | Exact control, local bindings, `int`, and input string | Only local `count` and `digit` | The rule pins the translated body and continuation, preserves every unmentioned cell, and is guarded by `isStr` and `digitCodes`. The independently proved `INNER-SPEC` establishes the same state change from the lower definition. |
| Sentence-expression bridge | Summarize the exact translated output expression | Exact expression, `count`, and builtin `str` | Only the computation value | The expression, argument order, and binding are pinned. `SENTENCE-SPEC` proves the concrete expression reaches the exact sentence representation using the lower sentence definition. |
| Outer-loop bridge | Summarize the exact translated list loop | Exact loop control/frame, input sequence, result reference, heap, `int`, and `str` | Result-list heap value and loop temporaries handled by cleanup | The rule pins the translated outer body and arbitrary preserved continuation. `OUTER-SPEC` proves it from the inner and sentence layers. |

The generated `solution-program.k` is derived from `solution.mpy`, which is
derived from `solution.py` by the fixed `py2mpy.py`. The full target claim loads
that generated program, rather than a handwritten substitute. The three
auxiliary claims independently connect each exact program fragment to the next
proof layer.

