1137:{"timestamp":"2026-07-22T04:02:06.198Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0de9a018f73772b0016a60409008c48196ba7dd257ed7cad8d","status":"completed","call_id":"call_yPzgLlzaKSka1UCn62dfUN22","name":"exec","input":"const p = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /work/PROOF.md\\n+VALIDATED\\n+\\n+# What is proven\\n+\\n+For every finite list `INPUT` of finite ASCII decimal-digit strings, calling\\n+`odd_count(INPUT)` returns a fresh list whose corresponding element is\\n+\\n+```text\\n+the number of odd elements Nn the strNng N of the Nnput.\\n+```\\n+\\n+where `N` is the number of odd digits in that input string. This is exactly the\\n+result of replacing every `i` in\\n+`the number of odd elements in the string i of the input.` with that count.\\n+The claim covers empty input lists, empty strings, repeated elements, and all\\n+ASCII digit strings of arbitrary finite length. It is a partial-correctness\\n+claim over the supplied reference semantics.\\n+\\n+# Formal claim\\n+\\n+The target claim is `ODD-COUNT-CORRECT` in module `SPEC` in `spec.k`. It starts\\n+from the supplied semantics' initial configuration, loads the exact translated\\n+`solutionProgram`, calls `odd_count` with `INPUT`, and proves that execution\\n+reaches `ref(0)` with heap location `0` containing\\n+`list(oddCountAppend(.ValSeq, INPUT))`, under `digitStrings(INPUT)`.\\n+\\n+`oddCountAppend` recursively maps each input string to `sentenceVal(N)`, with\\n+`N = oddDigits(codesOfVal(V))`. `sentenceVal(N)` is connected to the concrete\\n+runtime string by the guarded equality to `str(sentenceCodes(N))` in\\n+`VERIFICATION-SENTENCE-DEFINITION`; `sentenceCodes` is the exact ASCII encoding\\n+of the required replacement result.\\n+\\n+# Proof-extension inventory\\n+\\n+| Extension | Purpose | Reads | Writes | Justification |\\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n+| `oddBit`, `oddDigits` | Count odd ASCII digits | Mathematical integers and finite `IntSeq` values | None | Recursive equations decrease structurally; `oddBit(C) = (C - 48) mod 2` on the guarded digit domain. |\\n+| `codesOfVal`, `digitCodes`, `digitStrings` | State and enforce the formal input domain | Values and finite sequences | None | `codesOfVal` is total through its `owise` case; proof bridges require the positive string/digit guards. |\\n+| `sentenceCodes`, `sentenceVal` | Give an exact, compact proof representation of output strings | Count `N` | None | The final-only guarded rule applies after the fixed string expression evaluates and only when its concrete codes equal `sentenceCodes(N)`. Concretization is `sentenceVal(N) -> str(sentenceCodes(N))`. |\\n+| Inner-loop bridge | Summarize one exact translated inner digit loop | Exact control, local bindings, `int`, and input string | Only local `count` and `digit` | The rule pins the translated body and continuation, preserves every unmentioned cell, and is guarded by `isStr` and `digitCodes`. The independently proved `INNER-SPEC` establishes the same state change from the lower definition. |\\n+| Sentence-expression bridge | Summarize the exact translated output expression | Exact expression, `count`, and builtin `str` | Only the computation value | The expression, argument order, and binding are pinned. `SENTENCE-SPEC` proves the concrete expression reaches the exact sentence representation using the lower sentence definition. |\\n+| Outer-loop bridge | Summarize the exact translated list loop | Exact loop control/frame, input sequence, result reference, heap, `int`, and `str` | Result-list heap value and loop temporaries handled by cleanup | The rule pins the translated outer body and arbitrary preserved continuation. `OUTER-SPEC` proves it from the inner and sentence layers. |\\n+\\n+The generated `solution-program.k` is derived from `solution.mpy`, which is\\n+derived from `solution.py` by the fixed `py2mpy.py`. The full target claim loads\\n+that generated program, rather than a handwritten substitute. The three\\n+auxiliary claims independently connect each exact program fragment to the next\\n+proof layer.\\n+\\n+# Commands and observed results\\n+\\n+`prove.sh` is the exact reproducible runner. It regenerates all derived files,\\n+compiles the concrete and symbolic definitions, runs the concrete test, proves\\n+all positive claims, and checks both negative probes. Running:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+./prove.sh\\n+```\\n+\\n+exited `0`. Its principal commands and observed outcomes were:\\n+\\n+```sh\\n+python3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy\\n+python3 generate_program_module.py solution.mpy solutionProgram SOLUTION-PROGRAM > solution-program.k\\n+\\n+kompile reference-semantics/semantics.k \\\\\\n+  --main-module MPY-KRUN --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\\\\n+  --backend llvm --directory runtime-kompiled\\n+krun smoke.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled --output none\\n+# exit 0\\n+\\n+kompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION-BASE \\\\\\n+  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\\\\n+  --directory inner-verification-kompiled\\n+kprove spec.k --definition inner-verification-kompiled \\\\\\n+  --spec-module INNER-SPEC\\n+# #Top; exit 0\\n+\\n+kompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION-SENTENCE-DEFINITION \\\\\\n+  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\\\\n+  --directory sentence-verification-kompiled\\n+kprove spec.k --definition sentence-verification-kompiled \\\\\\n+  --spec-module SENTENCE-SPEC\\n+# #Top; exit 0\\n+\\n+kompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION-OUTER-BASE \\\\\\n+  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\\\\n+  --directory outer-verification-kompiled\\n+kprove spec.k --definition outer-verification-kompiled \\\\\\n+  --spec-module OUTER-SPEC\\n+# #Top; exit 0\\n+\\n+kompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION \\\\\\n+  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\\\\n+  --directory verification-kompiled\\n+kprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC\\n+# #Top; exit 0\\n+```\\n+\\n+The anti-vacuity false-postcondition probe was expected to fail and did fail\\n+with exit `1`; its residual state contained `sentenceVal(1)` where the probe\\n+demanded `sentenceVal(0)`. The implementation-mutation probe changes\\n+`count += int(digit) % 2` to `count += 0`; it was also expected to fail and did\\n+fail with exit `1`, exposing `sentenceVal(0)` where `sentenceVal(1)` was\\n+required. Both expected failures are enforced by `prove.sh`.\\n+\\n+The independent Python differential test reported:\\n+\\n+```text\\n+differential cases: 12111; mismatches: 0\\n+```\\n+\\n+It compares the implementation against a direct odd-digit oracle over all\\n+single ASCII digit strings of lengths zero through four and 1,000 deterministic\\n+random lists.\\n+\\n+# Gate results\\n+\\n+## Gate A — proof-extension soundness: PASS\\n+\\n+- Program identity is preserved through the generated translation and exact AST\\n+  aliases. The full proof loads this generated program. Replacing the target\\n+  update with a faulty body makes the target proof fail.\\n+- Each bridge pins the relevant control term, translated body or expression,\\n+  local-frame shape, builtin bindings, argument order, and continuation. Cells\\n+  omitted from a rewrite are preserved by K's configuration matching.\\n+- The inner bridge changes only `count` and `digit`; the expression bridge\\n+  changes only the computation; the outer bridge changes the result heap entry\\n+  and the loop temporaries consumed by its exact cleanup sequence.\\n+- All summary functions are total or used beneath explicit guards, and every\\n+  recursive equation descends on a finite sequence. No catch-all execution rule\\n+  can prove an exceptional or out-of-domain execution.\\n+- `INNER-SPEC`, `SENTENCE-SPEC`, and `OUTER-SPEC` separately prove the bridge\\n+  behavior over their lower-layer definitions. A deliberately false result is\\n+  rejected, supplying a concrete non-vacuity witness.\\n+\\n+## Gate B — theorem/intent alignment: PASS\\n+\\n+- The formal domain is finite lists of finite strings consisting only of ASCII\\n+  characters `0` through `9`, matching the intended HumanEval input class in\\n+  the supplied semantics. Empty lists and empty strings are included.\\n+- The postcondition computes the digit parity sum and the exact global\\n+  replacement text, not merely a length, type, or weak approximation.\\n+- The final return reference and its heap value are observable and constrained;\\n+  the input is not mutated. Temporary locals are internal implementation state.\\n+- Mathematical K integers agree with Python's unbounded integer behavior for\\n+  these counts. The ASCII restriction is explicit because the supplied string\\n+  semantics does not model the full CPython Unicode character universe.\\n+- Prompt examples, concrete `krun` execution, and the independent differential\\n+  oracle agree with the proved interpretation.\\n+\\n+## Gate C — validation completeness: PASS\\n+\\n+- All required sources, generated artifacts, exact commands, positive proof\\n+  results, and negative probe outcomes are present in the current directory.\\n+- The proof was rerun end-to-end by `prove.sh`; every positive `kprove` printed\\n+  `#Top` and exited `0`.\\n+- Both a false-postcondition probe and an implementation-body mutation are\\n+  rejected, and broad concrete differential testing supplies an independent\\n+  check of the specification interpretation.\\n+- The proof-local abstractions, their footprints, guards, concretization, and\\n+  composition boundaries are inventoried above rather than left implicit.\\n+\\n+# Trust boundary\\n+\\n+Trusted components are the supplied read-only `reference-semantics/`, the fixed\\n+`py2mpy.py` translator, the installed K toolchain (K/Haskell/LLVM backends and\\n+SMT integration), and ordinary correctness of the host process/filesystem.\\n+`solution.py`, the generated program module, all proof summaries and bridges,\\n+the specification, and the test/probe scripts are proof artifacts and are not\\n+silently assumed correct. `sentenceVal` is a proof-local representation with an\\n+explicit exact-code connection, not an opaque external primitive.\\n+\\n+# Empirically supported facts\\n+\\n+LLVM `krun` establishes that the translated implementation executes normally\\n+on the generated smoke examples under the supplied semantics. The 12,111-case\\n+differential test supports the correspondence between the natural-language\\n+replacement contract and the formal summary. These tests are corroborating\\n+evidence; the universal domain claim comes from the successful symbolic proof.\\n+\\n+# Excluded behavior\\n+\\n+Inputs containing non-string values, signs, whitespace, decimal points, or\\n+non-ASCII Unicode digits are outside the claim. Behavior on malformed inputs\\n+and exception behavior outside `digitStrings(INPUT)` is not proved. The theorem\\n+states partial correctness and does not separately claim a machine-checked\\n+termination theorem, although both recursive summaries and the implemented\\n+loops range over finite sequences.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(p));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f87ca-940f-7151-95aa-baf41ce30f12"}}}
