ct program fragment to the next\nproof layer.\n\n# Commands and observed results\n\n`prove.sh` is the exact reproducible runner. It regenerates all derived files,\ncompiles the concrete and symbolic definitions, runs the concrete test, proves\nall positive claims, and checks both negative probes. Running:\n\n```sh\n./prove.sh\n```\n\nexited `0`. Its principal commands and observed outcomes were:\n\n```sh\npython3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy\npython3 generate_program_module.py solution.mpy solutionProgram SOLUTION-PROGRAM > solution-program.k\n\nkompile reference-semantics/semantics.k \\\n  --main-module MPY-KRUN --syntax-module MPY-SYNTAX \\\n  --backend llvm --directory runtime-kompiled\nkrun smoke.mpy --definition runtime-kompiled --output none\n# exit 0\n\nkompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION-BASE \\\n  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\n  --directory inner-verification-kompiled\nkprove spec.k --definition inner-verification-kompiled \\\n  --spec-module INNER-SPEC\n# #Top; exit 0\n\nkompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION-SENTENCE-DEFINITION \\\n  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\n  --directory sentence-verification-kompiled\nkprove spec.k --definition sentence-verification-kompiled \\\n  --spec-module SENTENCE-SPEC\n# #Top; exit 0\n\nkompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION-OUTER-BASE \\\n  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\n  --directory outer-verification-kompiled\nkprove spec.k --definition outer-verification-kompiled \\\n  --spec-module OUTER-SPEC\n# #Top; exit 0\n\nkompile verification.k --main-module VERIFICATION \\\n  --syntax-module VERIFICATION-SYNTAX --backend haskell \\\n  --directory verification-kompiled\nkprove spec.k --definition verification-kompiled --spec-module SPEC\n# #Top; exit 0\n```\n\nThe anti-vacuity false-postcondition probe was expected to fail and did fail\nwith exit `1`; its residual state contained `sentenceVal(1)` where the probe\ndemanded `sentenceVal(0)`. The implementation-mutation probe changes\n`count += int(digit) % 2` to `count += 0`; it was also expected to fail and did\nfail with exit `1`, exposing `sentenceVal(0)` where `sentenceVal(1)` was\nrequired. Both expected failures are enforced by `prove.sh`.\n\nThe independent Python differential test reported:\n\n```text\ndifferential cases: 12111; mismatches: 0\n```\n\nIt compares the implementation against a direct odd-digit oracle over all\nsingle ASCII digit strings of lengths zero through four and 1,000 deterministic\nrandom lists.\n\n# Gate results\n\n## Gate A — proof-extension soundness: PASS\n\n- Program identity is preserved through the generated translation and exact AST\n  aliases. The full proof loads this generated program. Replacing the target\n  update with a faulty body makes the target proof fail.\n- Each bridge pins the relevant control term, translated body or expression,\n  local-frame shape, builtin bindings, argument order, and continuation. Cells\n  omitted from a rewrite are preserved by K's configuration matching.\n- The inner bridge changes only `count` and `digit`; the expression bridge\n  changes only the computation; the outer bridge changes the result heap entry\n  and the loop temporaries consumed by its exact cleanup sequence.\n- All summary functions are total or used beneath explicit guards, and every\n  recursive equation descends on a finite sequence. No catch-all execution rule\n  can prove an exceptional or out-of-domain execution.\n- `INNER-SPEC`, `SENTENCE-SPEC`, and `OUTER-SPEC` separately prove the bridge\n  behavior over their lower-layer definitions. A deliberately false result is\n  rejected, supplying a concrete non-vacuity witness.\n\n## Gate B — theorem/intent alignment: PASS\n\n- The formal domain is finite lists of finite strings consisting only of ASCII\n  characters `0` through `9`, matching the intended HumanEval input class in\n  the supplied semantics. Empty lists and empty 